berkeley-master.logic.r1: argue/equivocates: A1: "unconceived" is taken as the objects being unconceived at the very time one conceives them in A1 C1 and as representing the objects in thought as existing with no one conceiving them where conceive_unconceived is used else, a reader says: the argument holds only if they are one; taken apart, C (S4) does not follow (berkeley-master.logic.r1.sense1)
berkeley-master.logic.r1: argue/countermodel: C (S4): conceive_mind_indep = T, conceive_unconceived = T, conceive_unconceived_2 = F
